# Python Fundamentals: Syntax, Types, and Control Flow

> Python is the language most people meet first — and the one they
> keep reaching for. This lesson builds the foundation: how names bind
> to values, which types Python ships with, and how if, for, and
> while give every program its shape.

## 1. Why Python Reads Like Pseudocode

Python is **interpreted** (runs line by line), **dynamically typed**
(types are checked at run time, not declared in advance), and
deliberately **readable** — blocks are marked by indentation, not
braces. The result is code that often reads like a plain-English
plan, which is why Python dominates teaching, scripting, data
science, and AI work.

The price is speed and strictness: Python is typically slower than
compiled languages, and type mistakes surface only when the code
runs. What you save in keystrokes you pay back in discipline — tests
and type hints matter more, not less.

