## 2. Variables and Dynamic Typing

A **variable** in Python is a *name bound to an object* — not a box
with a fixed type. The type lives with the value; the name may be
rebound to anything, at any time.

```python
age = 21              # the name age -> int object 21
age = "twenty-one"    # now age -> a str; Python does not complain
print(type(age))      # <class 'str'>
```

[definition]
**Dynamic typing** means type checking happens at run time and names
carry no type of their own. This is not "untyped": every object has
a type; you simply declare none.
[/definition]

Names are `snake_case` by convention, are case-sensitive, and may
contain letters, digits, and underscores — but cannot start with a
digit and cannot be a keyword (`if`, `class`, `lambda`).

[warning]
Freedom has a cost: a typo like `total = totla + n` creates a *new*
variable instead of raising an error, and the mistake appears only
when the wrong value is finally used. Run code early, run it often.
[/warning]

## 3. The Core Built-in Types

Python ships with a small set of types that cover almost everything.

| Type | Examples | Mutable? | Typical use |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `int` | `42`, `-7`, unbounded size | No | Counting, indexes |
| `float` | `3.14`, `2e-3` | No | Measurement (approximate!) |
| `bool` | `True`, `False` | No | Decisions |
| `str` | `"hello"` | No | Text (immutable sequence) |
| `list` | `[1, 2, 3]` | Yes | Ordered collections that grow |
| `tuple` | `(1, 2, 3)` | No | Fixed records, dict keys |
| `dict` | `{"id": 7}` | Yes | Key-to-value lookup |
| `set` | `{1, 2, 3}` | Yes | Uniqueness, membership |
| `NoneType` | `None` | No | "Nothing here yet" |

@diagram tree id="python-type-family" layout="tree" aria="Python core types grouped into numeric, sequence, mapping, set, and other"
  "Python types" -> "Numeric"
  "Python types" -> "Sequence"
  "Python types" -> "Mapping"
  "Python types" -> "Set"
  "Numeric" -> "int, float, complex"
  "Sequence" -> "list, tuple, str, range"
  "Mapping" -> "dict"
  "Set" -> "set, frozenset"
@/diagram

[info]
Mutability is the hidden divider: `list`, `dict`, and `set` can
change in place; `int`, `float`, `str`, and `tuple` cannot. Section 8
shows how this single fact produces Python's most famous bug.
[/info]

## 4. Indentation Is Syntax: if / elif / else

In Python a **block** is defined by consistent indentation — four
spaces by convention. There are no braces to lean on, so the shape
of the code *is* the structure of the program.

```python
score = 76
if score >= 90:
    grade = "A"
elif score >= 75:
    grade = "B"
else:
    grade = "C"
print(grade)          # B
```

Conditions need no parentheses, and Python judges **truthiness**:
`0`, `0.0`, `""`, `[]`, `{}`, and `None` behave as false; almost
everything else behaves as true.

[important]
Never mix tabs and spaces — the file may *look* aligned, but Python
will raise a `TabError` or, worse, silently nest a block differently
than it appears. Configure your editor to insert four spaces.
[/important]

## 5. Loops: for and while

Python's `for` iterates over the *items of a collection*, not over
counters:

```python
for word in ["sun", "mon", "tue"]:
    print(word.upper())          # SUN  MON  TUE

for i in range(1, 6, 2):         # start 1, stop before 6, step 2
    print(i)                     # 1  3  5
```

`while` repeats as long as a condition holds — the right tool when
the number of repetitions is unknown in advance:

```python
n = 1
while n * n < 100:
    n += 1                       # there is no n++ in Python
print(n)                         # 10
```

[tip]
`break` leaves the loop now; `continue` skips to the next round. A
loop's `else` clause runs only if the loop finished *without* a
`break` — a clean way to say "searched everything, found nothing".
[/tip]

## 6. Collections Compared: list vs tuple vs dict vs set

Choosing the right container is half of writing good Python.

| Collection | Literal | Ordered? | Mutable? | Duplicates? | Shines at |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `list` | `[1, 2]` | Yes | Yes | Yes | Ordered data, append/pop |
| `tuple` | `(1, 2)` | Yes | No | Yes | Fixed records, dict keys |
| `dict` | `{"a": 1}` | Insertion order | Yes | Keys unique | O(1) lookup by key |
| `set` | `{1, 2}` | No | Yes | No | O(1) membership, dedup |

[example]
Roll number to name? Use a `dict`. A roster where repeats must
vanish? Use a `set`. Coordinates of a point that must never change?
Use a `tuple`. A shopping basket that grows and shrinks? Use a
`list`.
[/example]

## 7. List Comprehensions

A **list comprehension** builds a list from an iterable in one
declarative line — loop, filter, and transform at once:

```python
squares = [n * n for n in range(1, 6)]          # [1, 4, 9, 16, 25]
evens   = [n for n in range(10) if n % 2 == 0]  # [0, 2, 4, 6, 8]
lengths = {w: len(w) for w in ["hi", "hello"]}  # dict comprehension
unique  = {c for c in "banana"}                 # set comprehension
```

The general reading: *transform each item, for each item in the
iterable, if the condition holds*. Comprehensions exist for dicts and
sets too, and nest — though nesting past one level is a readability
debt.

[tip]
If a comprehension needs two `for`s and an `if` before you can
understand it, write a normal loop instead. Readability always wins;
the comprehension is a tool, not a badge.
[/tip]

## 8. Common Pitfalls Every Beginner Hits

| Pitfall | What happens | Fix |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Mutable default argument | One list shared across all calls | Default to `None`, create inside |
| `5 / 2` vs `5 // 2` | `/` gives `2.5`; `//` floors to `2` — and `-7 // 2` is `-4` | Pick deliberately |
| `is` vs `==` | `is` compares identity, not equality | Use `==`; keep `is` for `None` |
| `0.1 + 0.2` | Gives `0.30000000000000004` | Compare with `math.isclose` |
| Mutating while looping | Items get skipped in a shrinking list | Loop over a copy |

The mutable default deserves a full look — it is the classic
interview question:

```python
def add_item(item, basket=[]):    # BUG: evaluated once, at def time
    basket.append(item)
    return basket

def add_item_safe(item, basket=None):
    if basket is None:
        basket = []               # fresh list on every call
    basket.append(item)
    return basket
```

[warning]
The default value is created **once**, when the function is defined —
not on each call. Every caller who omits `basket` shares the *same*
list, so results leak across calls in ways that are miserable to
debug. This is why functions and modularity matter early: the bug
lives in the call convention, not the loop.
[/warning]

## 9. Quick Revision

| Concept | One-line recall |
| --- | --- |
| Dynamic typing | Types live on values and are checked at run time |
| Indentation | Four spaces define blocks — it is syntax, not style |
| Truthiness | `0`, `""`, `[]`, `{}`, `None` are false; most else is true |
| Container choice | list = ordered, tuple = fixed, dict = lookup, set = unique |
| Comprehension | transform for item in iterable, if condition |
| Mutable default | Never default to a mutable value — use `None` |
